'''Cinde Warmington''' (born December 7, 1957) is an American attorney, politician, and former [[Lobbying in the United States|lobbyist]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Porter |first=Steven |date=2023-06-14 |title=Lobbying work for Purdue Pharma plagues N.H. gubernatorial candidate |url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/2023/06/14/metro/lobbying-work-purdue-pharma-plagues-nh-gubernatorial-candidate/ |access-date=2026-08-06 |work=The Boston Globe |language=en-US |issn=0743-1791}}</ref> As a [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]], she served as a member of the [[Executive Council of New Hampshire]] from 2021 to 2025.

Prior to her election, Warmington worked in private practice as a healthcare attorney.<ref name=":1" /> She formerly worked as a lobbyist on behalf of defunct pharmaceutical company [[Purdue Pharma]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Mitropoulos |first=Arielle |date=2023-07-28 |title=Cinde Warmington faces criticism over past defenses of OxyContin, doctor who later surrendered license |url=https://www.wmur.com/article/cinde-warmington-opioid-crisis-criticism-governor/44676041# |access-date=2024-01-23 |website=WMUR}}</ref> In a 2026 statement, Warmington said she had been assigned the work as a junior attorney long before Purdue Pharma's fraudulent conduct was widely known. Citing her own family's experiences with addiction, she said she never would have accepted the work had she known of Purdue's fraud at the time.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Instagram |url=https://www.instagram.com/p/DbwJvghPS8u/ |access-date=2026-10-20 |website=www.instagram.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Landrigan |first=Kevin |date=2026-08-11 |title=Warmington: Purdue Pharma duped me into becoming opioid lawy... |url=https://www.unionleader.com/news/politics/warmington-purdue-pharma-duped-me-into-becoming-opioid-lawyer-lobbyist-03b97e1e |access-date=2026-10-20 |website=New Hampshire Union Leader |language=en}}</ref>

Warmington ran for [[Governor of New Hampshire]] in the [[2024 New Hampshire gubernatorial election|2024 election]], losing the Democratic primary to former [[Manchester, New Hampshire|Manchester]] mayor [[Joyce Craig]].<ref name=":4" /> She is running again in the [[2026 New Hampshire gubernatorial election|2026 election]], challenging incumbent [[Kelly Ayotte]].<ref name=":5" />

== Education ==
Warmington earned a Bachelor of Science degree in medical technology from the [[University of Massachusetts Dartmouth]], a [[Master of Business Administration]] from the [[University of Texas at Arlington]], and a [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of New Hampshire School of Law]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Councilor Cinde Warmington {{!}} District 2 {{!}} New Hampshire Executive Council |url=https://www.nh.gov/council/districts/d2/biography.htm |access-date=2022-07-24 |website=www.nh.gov}}</ref>

== Legal and lobbying career ==
Warmington began her career as a health care administrator. She has since worked as a health care attorney at Shaheen & Gordon, P.A. in the firm's health care practice group. She practiced health care law for 20 years.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2020-08-18 |title=Cinde Warmington, District 2 Executive Council Candidate |url=https://patch.com/new-hampshire/concord-nh/cinde-warmington-district-2-executive-council-candidate |access-date=2022-07-24 |website=Concord, NH Patch |language=en}}</ref>

In 2002, Warmington lobbied on behalf of [[Purdue Pharma]] in [[Concord, New Hampshire]], where she defended the company's record on [[Oxycodone|Oxycontin]] prescriptions. Describing it as a "miracle drug for many patients", Warmington argued the prescription pill "has very few side effects".<ref name=":2" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Porter |first=Steven |last3= |last4= |first4= |date=2023-06-14 |title=Lobbying work for Purdue Pharma plagues N.H. gubernatorial candidate |url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/2023/06/14/metro/lobbying-work-purdue-pharma-plagues-nh-gubernatorial-candidate/ |access-date=2024-01-24 |website=The Boston Globe |language=en-US}}</ref>

== Political career ==

=== Executive Council of New Hampshire ===
In 2020, she ran for the 2nd district of the [[Executive Council of New Hampshire]] to succeed fellow Democrat [[Andru Volinsky]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Democrat Cinde Warmington wins NH Executive Council District 2 race |url=https://www.seacoastonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/11/04/democrat-cinde-warmington-declares-win-nh-executive-council/6108487002/ |access-date=2022-07-24 |website=Portsmouth Herald |language=en-US}}</ref> Her campaign received support from [[Abortion-rights movements|pro-choice groups]] including [[EMILY's List|EMILY’s List]] and [[Planned Parenthood]]'s New Hampshire Action Fund PAC.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-08-06 |title=Pro-choice groups endorse Cinde Warmington for Executive Council |url=https://www.fosters.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/10/06/pro-choice-groups-endorse-cinde-warmington-for-executive-council/42727455/ |access-date=2024-01-24 |website=Foster's Daily Democrat |language=en-US}}</ref> She defeated her Republican opponent, Jim Beard, by a 54.4% to 45.5% margin.<ref name=":3" />

Warmington was reelected in 2022 by a 60% to 40% margin against Republican state senator [[Harold F. French]].<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |title=Cinde Warmington |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Cinde_Warmington |access-date=2022-07-24 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref> As a member of the Executive Council, Warmington urged colleagues to vote down efforts to cancel state contracts with [[Planned Parenthood]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Towfighi |first=Michaela |date=2023-11-25 |title=Following success of other states, Warmington and Craig campaign on abortion |url=https://www.concordmonitor.com/GovernorsRaceDemocratsAbortionStances-52953811 |access-date=2024-01-24 |website=Concord Monitor}}</ref>

=== 2024 gubernatorial campaign ===
In June 2023, she announced her campaign for [[Governor of New Hampshire]] in the [[2024 New Hampshire gubernatorial election|2024 election]].<ref name=":0">{{cite news |last1=Bookman |first1=Todd |title=Executive Councilor Cinde Warmington announces campaign for NH governor |url=https://www.nhpr.org/politics/2023-06-01/executive-councilor-cinde-warmington-announces-campaign-for-nh-governor |access-date=3 July 2023 |publisher=New Hampshire Public Radio |date=June 1, 2023}}</ref> In December 2023, her campaign reported that it brought it over $1 million in donations since she announced her candidacy.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Graham |first=Michael |date=2023-12-06 |title=Warmington Announces 'Record' $1 Million Haul in Dem. Gubernatorial Primary |url=https://nhjournal.com/warmington-announces-record-1-million-haul-in-dem-gubernatorial-primary/ |access-date=2024-01-24 |website=NH Journal |language=en-US}}</ref> Warmington stated that if elected, her inaugural budget proposal would seek to address housing affordability, childcare, and education.<ref>{{Cite web |last=DeWitt |first=Ethan |date=2023-09-19 |title=Governor's race 2024: Here's where the four candidates stand on the issues |url=https://newhampshirebulletin.com/2023/09/19/governors-race-2024-heres-where-the-four-candidates-stand-on-the-issues/ |access-date=2024-01-24 |website=New Hampshire Bulletin |language=en-US}}</ref>

During the campaign, Warmington made combating the [[Opioid epidemic in the United States|opioid epidemic]] in New Hampshire a leading campaign issue. She has received scrutiny over her work on behalf of PMC Medical Group, a network of pain management clinics accused of contributing to the state's opioid crisis.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Landrigan |first=Kevin |date=2023-12-02 |title=State House Dome: Warmington facing more coverage over ties to opioid firms |url=https://www.unionleader.com/news/politics/statehouse_dome/state-house-dome-warmington-facing-more-coverage-over-ties-to-opioid-firms/article_9d0ee69c-456b-53b6-9fe6-ca13870f0758.html |access-date=2024-01-24 |website=UnionLeader.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Lahut |first=Jake |date=2023-07-04 |title=She's Running on the Opioid Issue. Her History Is a Problem. |url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/cinde-warmington-is-running-on-the-opioid-epidemic-in-new-hampshire-she-lobbied-for-the-company-behind-it |access-date=2024-01-24 |work=The Daily Beast |language=en}}</ref> One of its providers was convicted for taking illegal kickbacks, eventually leading to Owner Michael J. O'Connell surrendering his medical license. Warmington represented O'Connell after he surrendered his license, helped negotiate a settlement for a doctor tied to the kickback scandal, lobbied against legislation that would prevent doctors with revoked licenses from owning a medical practice, and pressured local media outlets against running stories critical of PMC.<ref>{{Cite web| title=Settlement Agreement | url=https://big.assets.huffingtonpost.com/athena/files/2023/12/01/65698e01e4b07b937ff44268.pdf | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231202035721/https://big.assets.huffingtonpost.com/athena/files/2023/12/01/65698e01e4b07b937ff44268.pdf | archive-date=2023-12-02}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web| title=Settlement Agreement | url=https://big.assets.huffingtonpost.com/athena/files/2023/12/01/65698fd0e4b07b937ff442d0.pdf | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231201182358/https://big.assets.huffingtonpost.com/athena/files/2023/12/01/65698fd0e4b07b937ff442d0.pdf | archive-date=2023-12-01}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Rogers |first=Josh |date=2024-08-29 |title=Attacks over opioid lobbying rise to fore in Democratic race for NH governor |url=https://www.nhpr.org/nh-news/2024-08-29/attacks-over-opioid-lobbying-rise-to-fore-in-democratic-race-for-nh-governor |access-date=2026-08-06 |website=New Hampshire Public Radio |language=en}}</ref>

O'Connell later donated $49,000 to Warmington's campaign.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Reporter |first=Daniel MaransSenior Politics |last2=HuffPost |date=2023-12-01 |title=New Hampshire Democratic Contender Got Big Donations From Pain Clinic At Heart Of State's Opioid Crisis |url=https://www.huffpost.com/entry/cinde-warmington-paincare-donations-new-hampshire_n_656993d5e4b07b937ff443f5 |access-date=2026-08-06 |website=HuffPost |language=en}}</ref> Recovery advocates and first responders have described it as "blood money".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Buckminster |first=Sofie |date=2024-06-04 |title=Recovery advocates urge Cinde Warmington to drop out of governor's race |url=https://www.concordmonitor.com/2024/06/04/cinde-warmington-called-to-exit-race-by-oxycontin-survivors-55437355/ |access-date=2026-08-06 |website=Concord Monitor |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-09-07 |title=The Soapbox: 'I don't buy what Cinde Warmington is saying' - Manchester Ink Link |url=https://manchester.inklink.news/the-soapbox-i-dont-buy-what-cinde-warmington-is-saying/ |access-date=2026-08-06 |language=en-US}}</ref>

[[Kathy Sullivan (American politician)|Kathy Sullivan]], the former chair of the [[New Hampshire Democratic Party]], expressed concerns regarding her work for the opioid industry, telling voters that "the dangers of opioids were known - that's why the legislature was acting."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Marans |first=Daniel |date=2023-12-01 |title=New Hampshire Democratic Contender Got Big Donations From Pain Clinic At Heart Of State's Opioid Crisis |url=https://www.huffpost.com/entry/cinde-warmington-paincare-donations-new-hampshire_n_656993d5e4b07b937ff443f5 |access-date=2024-01-24 |website=HuffPost |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2024-08-26 |title=Kathy Sullivan (@NHKathySullivan) on X |url=https://x.com/NHKathySullivan/status/1828128323157008846?s=42 |access-date=2026-08-06 |work=X (formerly Twitter) |language=en-US}}</ref>

Warmington ultimately came in second in the primary election, finishing behind former [[Manchester, New Hampshire|Manchester]] mayor [[Joyce Craig]], and ahead of Jon Kiper.<ref name=":4">{{cite web |title=New Hampshire Governor Primary |url=https://apnews.com/projects/election-results-2024/new-hampshire/?r=30641 |publisher=[[Associated Press]] |access-date=12 September 2024}}</ref>

=== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ===
In February 2026, she announced that she would be launching another campaign for governor, this time in the [[2026 New Hampshire gubernatorial election|2026 election]].<ref name=":5">{{cite web |last1=Rogers |first1=Josh |title=Cinde Warmington launches second bid for New Hampshire governor |url=https://www.nhpr.org/nh-news/2026-02-18/cinde-warmington-running-new-hampshire-governor-2026 |website=nhpr.org |publisher=New Hampshire Public Radio |access-date=19 February 2026 |date=18 February 2026}}</ref>

More than twice as many voters view Warmington unfavorably as view her favorably.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=UNH Survey Center |date=2026-06-30 |title=Races Tighten in New Hampshire for Governor and U.S. Senate 6/30/2026 |url=https://scholars.unh.edu/survey_center_polls/974 |journal=All UNH Survey Center Polls}}</ref>

Warmington's 2026 campaign is raising significantly less money than her 2024 campaign.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Graham |first=Michael |date=2026-06-18 |title=Weak Fundraising Numbers Add to Warmington's Woes |url=https://nhjournal.com/weak-fundraising-numbers-add-to-warmingtons-woes/ |access-date=2026-08-06 |website=NH Journal |language=en-US}}</ref>

== Political positions ==
As a gubernatorial candidate, Warmington advocated for expanding access to [[abortion in New Hampshire]], and favors repealing the 24-week abortion ban instituted by Governor [[Chris Sununu]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sexton |first=Adam |date=2023-09-08 |title=Warmington, a Democrat running for governor, calls for repeal of 24-week abortion ban in NH |url=https://www.wmur.com/article/new-hampshire-democrat-cinde-warmington-abortion/45054634 |access-date=2024-01-24 |website=WMUR |language=en}}</ref> Warmington has identified the state's housing crisis and the opioid crisis as long-term challenges facing New Hampshire residents.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Green |first=Rick |date=2023-06-03 |title=Warmington highlights housing, fentanyl epidemic, health care in bid for governor |url=https://www.sentinelsource.com/news/local/statehouse/warmington-highlights-housing-fentanyl-epidemic-health-care-in-bid-for-governor/article_76b48ebe-6019-5261-ab18-8ee3376a653a.html |access-date=2024-01-24 |website=SentinelSource.com |language=en}}</ref>

Warmington supports a ban on Natural Gas in New Hampshire by 2040. She has not announced the anticipated cost for mandated solar and wind.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Graham |first=Michael |date=2026-07-07 |title=Warmington To Democrats: I'm Bringing Back My Net Zero Energy Plan |url=https://nhjournal.com/warmington-to-democrats-im-bringing-back-my-net-zero-energy-plan/ |access-date=2026-08-06 |website=NH Journal |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sexton |first=Adam |date=2026-08-03 |title=Cinde Warmington says no to more fossil fuel energy supply {{!}} CloseUp |url=https://www.wmur.com/article/cinde-warmington-says-no-to-fossil-fuel-energy-closeup/73335104 |access-date=2026-08-06 |website=WMUR |language=en}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Warmington is married to Bill Christie, an attorney who was legal counsel for the [[New Hampshire Democratic Party]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-08-18 |title=Cinde Warmington, District 2 Executive Council Candidate |url=https://patch.com/new-hampshire/concord-nh/cinde-warmington-district-2-executive-council-candidate |access-date=2024-11-19 |website=Concord, NH Patch |language=en}}</ref>

